- **`noninteractive' is two things in GNU and one in Emaxx (round 364's review).**  print.c, xdisp.c and the rest of GNU's C read the C flag `noninteractive', which emacs.c sets once from the command line; the Lisp variable of that name is `noninteractive1', copied from the flag at startup, and binding it changes nothing C does.  Emaxx reads the Lisp variable at each of those places (109 sites: PrintSink::of, `message', `terpri', the command loop's and the process code's batch branches), so under `(let ((noninteractive nil)) ...)' in a batch session `princ' writes to the echo area and the log where GNU writes stdout, and under `(let ((noninteractive t)) ...)' in a terminal session the reverse.  A round of its own: one flag set at startup, read by what reads GNU's, the variable left to Lisp.
- **What print.c's undefined cases give is not reproduced (round 364).**  Where GNU reads memory that is no part of the text, Emaxx stops or stores a raw byte and the results differ from one GNU run to the next: a string in `print-number-table' whose unibyte data is printed into a multibyte buffer (bytes that are no character; `buffer-string' then reads past them); `write-char' of a negative code whose low byte is not ASCII into a multibyte buffer (the same: Emaxx stores that byte's raw-byte character); the same code to `external-debugging-output' or through `standard-display-table' with a coding system when its two-byte form is no character's (GNU encodes a string that ends inside a character; Emaxx writes the two bytes); a string made shorter by more than its terminating NUL while `princ' or `prin1' walks it (Emaxx stops at the NUL); a print run by a before-change hook of print_finish's insertion that makes print_buffer grow (insert_1_both holds the freed block; Emaxx inserts the text it held) or whose partial copy ends inside a multibyte character of the waiting text (GNU inserts bytes that are no character with the old counts; Emaxx a raw byte each); a print that goes on, after a print run from inside it, from the `pos' that print left inside a multibyte character of its own text (the same bytes that are no character: GNU's buffer reads `(c<obarray n=0>)' where `(é #<obarray n=0>)' was printed with a `print-unreadable-function' that calls `prin1-to-string', in two of the fuzz programs; Emaxx's reads the raw byte and the `#'); the point print_finish sets past the end of the buffer a killed destination left the print in (Emaxx clamps it); a print stack entry of a print left by a non-local exit whose object has since been freed (GNU aborts in one of the reviewer's programs, status 134); and a print run by a function printed to that makes the print stack grow while the outer print holds a pointer to its entry (xpalloc moves the stack, the outer entry's step for that element is written to the freed block, and GNU prints the element twice: `[(1 2 2 3' and `(p 1 1)' in two of the fuzz programs; Emaxx's stack has no such pointer; the 20 fuzz programs that differ so agree once a print of an 80-deep list has grown GNU's stack beforehand, two of them where GNU otherwise dies of SIGSEGV); and a print run whole from inside another, which resets print_depth, so that the outer print's own decrements take it below zero and print.c stores at `being_printed[print_depth]' before the array (in the oracle's build index -4 is `printchar_stdout_last', so a later `terpri' with ENSURE answers by the object stored there, and lower ones are print_buffer's fields; Emaxx keeps the depth signed and stores nothing).  The negative codes' bytes themselves (low byte alone in printchar, the two-byte arithmetic in printchar_to_stream) are what the oracle's build makes of CHAR_STRING where `eassume (0 <= c)' does not hold, and are reproduced as that build gives them; a function is handed a C string's bytes as `char' is signed on the host, as GNU's `int ch = ptr[i]' is.
- **The echo area is not a buffer (round 364's review).**  GNU's print to t in an interactive session inserts into the echo area buffer (` *Echo Area 0*' or 1), current after setup_echo_area_for_printing, and Lisp can read it there; Emaxx keeps the echo area's text apart and those buffers stay empty.  The second review's two cases are of it: a print to t of a character a Rust `char' cannot hold reaches `current-message' and `*Messages*' as another (`(princ (string #x110000 ?a #x3fff7f))' gives 63743 97 63743, and the private-use characters from #xe080 to #xe0ff come back as raw bytes), since `deliver' hands the echo area host text; and after `(kill-buffer (messages-buffer))' a `princ' that starts with a strout leaves `current-message' nil in GNU, whose strout logs (and makes `*Messages*' again) before it inserts.  One case of the first review is not understood: an uncaught error from `(princ "é")' under `standard-display-table' with an unknown `coding-system-for-write' starts GNU's stderr with one more newline than Emaxx's.
- **The obarray holds fewer symbols than GNU's (seen in round 364's fuzz).**  `#<obarray n=18160>' for GNU's `obarray' at the start of a batch session is `n=17837' in Emaxx: the symbols GNU's C interns that Emaxx's does not (the oracle is an X build; which of the difference is that and which is missing DEFSYMs is not yet counted).
- **Primitives read variables through the lexical environment (seen in round 364).**  GNU's C reads a DEFVAR as the C variable it is and any other variable with Fsymbol_value: the dynamic value.  Only the evaluator's own reference to a variable looks in the lexical environment.  Emaxx's primitives are handed the caller's environment and most read variables through it (523 `lookup_var_key (cached_symbol! ...)' sites and 42 `lookup_var' ones outside the tests), so an entry of a special variable's name there (in the alist `eval' is given, or an interpreted closure's) is taken for the variable: `(eval '(read "#1=(a . #1#)") '((read-circle . nil)))' signals invalid-read-syntax where GNU reads the circle, `(eval '(expand-file-name "x") '((default-directory . "/nonexistent/")))' expands under that directory, `(eval '(intern-soft "car") '((obarray . [])))' signals, and `current-column' takes `tab-width' from it (4 of 24 such forms tried; round 363 answers the same).  Round 364 made the printer's reads dynamic: its stream (`standard-output'), the batch flag where the printer and `terpri' read it, and the print variables.  The rest is a round of its own: each site either a C read (the dynamic value, a buffer-local one where GNU's is per buffer) or the evaluator's.
- **Interpreted code and the call from C to a Lisp function are slower than GNU's (measured in round 364).**  The round's timing probes are user plus system seconds, least of five, startup included.  Printing to stdout, into a string and long strings and lists are at or under GNU (PERF364).  Two things outside the printer show in them.  An interpreted loop of four `(ignore X)' forms 1,500,000 times takes 0.39 s against GNU's 0.29 (1.37): eval_sub's own work for a form in source.  And a print to a function, a call of an interpreted one-argument lambda for each of 12,800,000 characters, takes PERFFUNC: under callgrind the printer's own part is smaller than round 363's (350 million instructions of 2,042 million against 406 of 2,063 for a tenth of the run) and what is left is Interpreter::call_function_value and call_function_value_general, some 200 instructions a call before funcall_interpreted_lambda begins.  Round 363 took less time there only because it made 6 collections where GNU makes 14: it did not make the copy print_prune_string_charset makes of each string `prin1' prints, which round 364 makes (15 collections, and `memory-use-counts' as GNU's for it).  Both belong to the performance contract's work on calls, not to the printer.
- **print_preprocess numbers a string's intervals in the order of positions; GNU's in its interval tree's (round 364's second review).**  print_preprocess_string runs under traverse_intervals_noorder, which visits a tree's root, then its left subtree, then its right, so with `print-circle' the labels of values shared between a string's property lists follow the shape of the tree: for two one-character pieces each with `k' and `j' the same list, GNU prints `#("xx" 0 1 (j #2=(1) k #2#) 1 2 (j #1=(2) k #1#))' and Emaxx numbers them 1 then 2; for seven pieces GNU's labels are 3 2 4 1 6 5 7.  Emaxx's intervals are a sequence with no tree, so the order cannot be told from them: it needs intervals.c's tree and its balancing (balance_intervals, the rotations of split and merge) kept as GNU keeps them.  Round 363 answers the same.
- **DISP_CHAR_VECTOR outside the printer (round 364's second review).**  The review found `standard-display-table' looked up with CHAR_TABLE_REF where print.c uses DISP_TABLE_P and DISP_CHAR_VECTOR (an ASCII character is answered by the table's own ASCII slot, never its parent, and by the default only while no ASCII character has an entry; six extra slots are required), and round 364 gave the char table those two (CharTableRef::is_display_table, disp_char_vector) for the printer's stdout and stderr.  The redisplay and motion code still reads display tables with CHAR_TABLE_REF: tty.rs (2 sites), indent_motion.rs's display_vector, text.rs and values.rs (one each), where a parent table's ASCII entries and a default beside an ASCII entry are shown that GNU does not show.  To be moved to the same two functions with terminal tests of their own.
